Keep a packet's presentation time when movenc moves its decode time forward.

check_pkt() moves a packet whose decode time is earlier than the end of the
previous sample to just after it, and until now also threw the packet's
presentation time away. A sample with no presentation time is then given its
decode time instead, so a frame shown after it is decoded loses its whole
reorder delay and is presented early, over the frames before it.

In fragmented output this happens whenever a fragment's last sample says it
lasts longer than it really does: its end is taken from AVPacket.duration, so
the next fragment's first sample looks as though it goes backwards. Matroska
files that give the frame before a keyframe a two-frame BlockDuration do this
at every keyframe, and an HLS fMP4 remux of them shows a keyframe four frames
early at most segment starts, which players present as a skip or a stale frame.

Keep the presentation time whenever it is still at or after the corrected
decode time, which is all a sample needs. Only when it would now come before
it is it dropped as before. Nothing changes for a stream that never takes
this branch.

Index: FFmpeg/libavformat/movenc.c
===================================================================
--- FFmpeg.orig/libavformat/movenc.c
+++ FFmpeg/libavformat/movenc.c
@@ -6742,7 +6742,8 @@
                duration, pkt->dts, pkt->stream_index);
 
         pkt->dts = ref + 1;
-        pkt->pts = AV_NOPTS_VALUE;
+        if (pkt->pts != AV_NOPTS_VALUE && pkt->pts < pkt->dts)
+            pkt->pts = AV_NOPTS_VALUE;
     }
 
     if (pkt->duration < 0 || pkt->duration > INT_MAX) {
